# Four to a beat: semiquavers counted in longer notes, a written bar added, a bar filled, two bars
# tapped, what the second beam means, and two bars of the child's own for the canal town's mill
# wheel.
lesson music-semiquavers v=1 format=teach grade=5 unit=3 subject=music levels=[easy, medium, hard] {
  title "Four to a beat: semiquavers"
  goal "Count, write and tap semiquavers, four to a beat, beside quavers and crotchets."

  look {
    scene 36x12 {
      notes eg clef=treble notes=[G4, G4, G4, G4, G4, G4, C5, C5] values=[0.25, 0.25, 0.25, 0.25, 0.5, 0.5, 1, 1] meter=4 letters=false at=canvas(1, 1)
    }
    say """
      The mill by the canal turns its wheel fast, and the miller's tune runs to keep up. A crotchet
      is one beat; a quaver, with one beam, is half a beat; a semiquaver, with two beams, is a
      quarter of a beat, so four semiquavers fill one beat. Count them ti-ka-ti-ka, or 1 e and a.
      The bar here has a beat of four semiquavers, a beat of two quavers and two crotchets. Each
      beam added halves the note again.
      """
  }
  do {
    show music.semi-many k=0 easy-k=0 hard-k=2
    show music.semi-count v=1 easy-v=0 hard-v=1
    level easy {
      worked music.semi-fill v=0 level=medium
    }
    show music.semi-tap
    show music.semi-fill v=1 hard-v=2
    show music.semi-many k=1 easy-k=1 hard-k=3
    show music.semi-tap-mix
    level medium hard {
      show music.semi-count v=2
      show music.semi-fill v=3 hard-v=3
    }
    level easy {
      show music.semi-count v=1
      show music.semi-many k=2
    }
    level hard {
      show music.semi-many k=1 level=medium
    }
  }
  try stars=2 {
    show music.semi-why r=0 easy-r=1
  }
  try stars=3 {
    show music.semi-write v=1 easy-v=0 hard-v=2
  }
  remember {
    say "A semiquaver has two beams and lasts a quarter of a beat: four fill one beat."
  }

  grown-ups """
    The idea is that each beam halves a note, so a semiquaver is a quarter of a beat and four fill
    one beat, and that a beat can be filled many ways: four semiquavers, two quavers, a quaver and
    two semiquavers. The step up from grade 2's quavers and grade 5's syncopation is a note half as
    long again, counted inside the beat. The wrong ideas to watch for are counting semiquavers as
    quavers, which halves every answer, and rushing the quick notes so they spill over the beat. In
    the working, look for the child grouping short notes into whole beats before adding. The core
    counts semiquavers in longer notes, adds a bar, fills a bar and taps two bars, checked against
    the beat; the two-star asks what the second beam means, and the three-star is two bars of the
    child's own, written and tapped, which you look at and listen to with the notice list.

    If the quarters are stuck, say "straw-ber-ry-jam" for four semiquavers and "ap-ple" for two
    quavers while tapping a beat. Off the page, tap four quick taps on every beat of a song you
    know, then two, then one. This lesson meets Russia's federal work programme in music for primary
    school, which lists semiquavers among the rhythms to learn, 「Шестнадцатые」 (as quoted in the
    audit of grades 5 and 6), and Japan's 2017 course of study for music, 第3 2(9), the notes and
    rests to understand and use as they work in music.
    """
  level easy {
    grown-ups "At this level the semiquavers are counted in a crotchet, a minim and a dotted crotchet, the bars to add have semiquavers beside quavers and crotchets, filling a bar is worked once in the teacher's pen, the bars to fill leave whole beats, the two-star is as written with its answers in another order, and the two bars each have one beat of semiquavers."
  }
  level hard {
    grown-ups "At this level the semiquavers are counted in a dotted crotchet and a bar of three-four, the bars to add mix semiquavers with dotted notes, the bars to fill leave quarters of a beat, and the two bars use a dotted quaver and semiquaver and a rest."
  }
}
